WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.814
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.419 --> 00:00:05.460
TODD KERPELMAN: I'm Todd.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:06.390
This is Arun.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.580
And we're glad
you could join us.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.040
Let's talk a lot about
Firebase Analytics.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.680
So I'm guessing many of
you are app developers,

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.450
and you're wondering
how Firebase can

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.550
make your life a little easier.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.650
Maybe you are
BigQuery aficionados

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.620
and you're wondering how you can
put your elite BigQuery skills

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.670
to use in a Firebase world.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:26.670
Or maybe you just wanted
somewhere kind of quiet

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.715
to check your email,
and that's fine too.

00:00:28.715 --> 00:00:29.340
We won't judge.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:30.630
From up here, it kind
of looks like you're

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:31.710
taking really good notes.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.440
So keep doing it.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.730
So I'm going to start by
explaining what Firebase is--

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:39.105
because I think a lot of people
still think of it as just

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:41.040
a real-time database,
and honestly Firebase

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.709
is a whole lot more than that.

00:00:42.709 --> 00:00:44.250
It's really a whole
suite of services

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.790
available on iOS and
Android and the web

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:51.090
designed to make it easier for
you to build successful apps.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:53.640
And these include everything
from a cross-platform

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.060
Notification Service with
Firebase Cloud Messaging

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.650
to Remote Config,
our fun little tool

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:00.900
kit that lets you change the
look and feel of your app

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:01.890
from the cloud.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.765
I don't know if you've
heard about the cloud,

00:01:03.765 --> 00:01:05.946
but I think it's going
to be big, mark my words.

00:01:05.946 --> 00:01:09.049
To-- yes, the Realtime
Database, to a whole bunch

00:01:09.049 --> 00:01:10.590
of other features
that I'm not really

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.910
going to talk about today
because at the heart of all

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:15.540
these features is this thing
here, Firebase Analytics.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.250
That's why we drew it
in the center like.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:20.280
It's like a visual metaphor.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.160
So I think anybody who's
worked on a successful app

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.950
knows that analytics are
really important to building

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:26.880
on that success.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.421
It's really the best way to find
out what people are actually

00:01:29.421 --> 00:01:30.450
doing within your app.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.930
And by finding out what users
are doing within your app,

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:34.846
you can make it better,
like, you can find out

00:01:34.846 --> 00:01:36.699
places where users
are getting stuck,

00:01:36.699 --> 00:01:38.490
if they're features
that they're not using,

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.040
or if there's parts
of your app that

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:43.185
are way more interesting or
sticky than you anticipated.

00:01:43.185 --> 00:01:45.060
And then you can adjust
your app accordingly,

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.730
according to what your
users are actually doing.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.190
So there are a lot of
libraries out there designed

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.790
to help developers
measure what's

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:53.400
going on within their app.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.790
Google Analytics was one
of the earliest of these,

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:57.716
but it was really targeted
more for websites.

00:01:57.716 --> 00:01:59.340
And you can kind of
see that in the way

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.260
it was designed, in
the way it's set up.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:04.320
It's got a lot of web page-y
terminology in the reports

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.870
and in the way it
processes information.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.210
It also limits you to about
10 million hits a month

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:11.760
before you need to upgrade
to an Analytics 360

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.330
subscription, which we know for
a lot of small indie developers

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.610
might be a bit of an issue.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.350
Firebase Analytics,
on the other hand,

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.530
was built from the ground
up to be mobile-centric.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:25.800
It comes at no cost to you and
supports an unlimited volume

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.530
of hits, no matter how many
millions upon millions of users

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:29.940
your app might have.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:31.440
So from our
perspective, this really

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.630
is the way that
mobile app developers

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:36.711
should be measuring what's
going on within their app.

00:02:36.711 --> 00:02:39.210
So let's take a closer look at
how Firebase Analytics treats

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.043
data, because if you
are going to be dealing

00:02:41.043 --> 00:02:43.230
with this data as a
BigQuery developer,

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:46.500
it helps to know what it looks
like underneath the hood.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.242
So Firebase Analytics is
really all about events.

00:02:49.242 --> 00:02:51.450
Nearly everything you want
to record within your app,

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.491
whether it's a user opening
it for the first time

00:02:53.491 --> 00:02:55.880
or making in-app purchase,
visiting your store

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.810
or completing a level is going
to be recorded as an event.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.400
Events have names.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.460
And then they also have
up to 25 key value pairs

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:07.102
or parameters that are
passed along with them.

00:03:07.102 --> 00:03:08.810
And these parameters
can be used to store

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.910
any kind of interesting
data about the event

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:14.150
that you as an app developer
might want to know.

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:15.800
Some events within
Firebase Analytics

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.320
are recorded and submitted
automatically for you

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:19.580
by the mobile libraries.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.740
These are things like
in-app purchases,

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.650
or the user updating
their operating system,

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:26.780
or your users opening your
app for the first time

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:27.420
on the library.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.360
It can automatically
detect these events

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:30.684
and submit them for you.

00:03:30.684 --> 00:03:32.600
Then there's a whole
bunch of suggested events

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.430
that are predefined
by Firebase Analytics,

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:35.930
and these tend to
be events that are

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.420
fairly common to a number
of different apps--

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:41.420
things like adding an item to
a card in an e-commerce app

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:43.370
or conducting a search.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.590
Now, unlike the automatic
events on the previous slide,

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:48.710
you'll need to submit
these events yourself.

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:50.540
And don't worry about the
tiny font for those of you

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:52.831
taking pictures, hoping your
resolution is good enough.

00:03:52.831 --> 00:03:55.852
We have documentation
that lists all of these.

00:03:55.852 --> 00:03:57.810
But I think the majority
of the events that you

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:00.730
will be sending within Firebase
Analytics are custom events.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:04.080
These are events that you define
specifically for your app,

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.150
and you can define up
to 500 different types

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.119
of custom events.

00:04:07.119 --> 00:04:09.660
And really, this can be anything
that's relevant to your app.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.720
Maybe that's like completing
a workout in a fitness app,

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.640
or unlocking an
achievement in a game,

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:18.160
or viewing a recipe
in a cooking app.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.244
And that's basically
events in a nutshell.

00:04:20.244 --> 00:04:21.660
The other important
type of data I

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.180
want to cover for Firebase
Analytics is user properties,

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:27.720
and these are well, properties
associated with a user, hence

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:28.884
the name.

00:04:28.884 --> 00:04:30.300
So the idea behind
user properties

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.060
is that as the user interacts
with your app over time,

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:34.520
you might learn more about them.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.270
You might learn what
their fitness goal is

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:37.740
for those of you
with fitness apps,

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:40.110
or what their favorite musical
genre is for those of you

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.060
with music apps, or
how they pronounce

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.630
gif, which as we can see
here is the correct way.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:48.450
And so the idea here is that
you can use this information

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:50.820
to filter your events by
these user properties.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.490
Like, maybe you're curious
if your yoga enthusiasts

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:55.610
are spending more
money within your app

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:57.270
than your weightlifters.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:59.610
And so you can go ahead and
filter your in-app purchase

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:02.709
event by this fitness
goal user property,

00:05:02.709 --> 00:05:04.500
and you can start to
see for yourself who's

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:07.083
spending more money in your app,
the weightlifters or the yoga

00:05:07.083 --> 00:05:07.620
people.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:09.990
And then maybe you can
adjust your marketing

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:12.250
spend accordingly.

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:13.570
And that's basically it.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:15.400
Just by using events
and user properties

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.710
you're able to supply
Firebase Analytics with all

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:19.210
the information it
needs to give you

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:21.430
some pretty useful
information about your app

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:23.510
in the form of some very
nice-looking graphs.

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:26.882
So in fact, let's take a look
at some of these right now.

00:05:26.882 --> 00:05:28.590
So these are some of
the basic dashboards

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:30.810
you'll get, essentially,
without any other work

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:32.580
as soon as you integrate
the Firebase SDK.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:38.840
So here we've got active users.

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:40.780
These are users
who are interacting

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:43.090
with your app over a
defined time period,

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:45.620
in our case the last 30 days.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:47.780
We have average revenue
per user, known as ARPU,

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.210
and average revenue per
paying user, known as ARPPU,

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:52.540
which is my favorite
acronym ever.

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:54.290
We have retention
cohorts, which shows you

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:56.880
how frequently people come
back to your app over time.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.865
And this is usually broken up
into weekly groups or cohorts.

00:06:00.865 --> 00:06:02.240
We have user
engagement, how much

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:03.656
time people spend
within their app

00:06:03.656 --> 00:06:05.020
when they're actually using it.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:06.222
Your top in-app purchases.

00:06:06.222 --> 00:06:07.680
And if we scroll
down a little bit,

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.640
we can see who your users
are, where they coming from,

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:12.570
and a whole bunch of
other useful information.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:14.570
But actually I don't want
to spend a lot of time

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:16.195
on these initial
dashboards because I'd

00:06:16.195 --> 00:06:19.260
like to spend a little more
time talking about events.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:20.780
So any event that
your app records,

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:23.930
whether it's a Firebase
automatic event or a custom

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:26.880
event that you're logging, shows
up here on our events panel.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.880
And if we were to click
through on one of these,

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:31.730
we could start to see more
details about this event

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:33.050
as it occurs within our app.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:36.140
So let's take a look at
three of these examples.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:39.260
This first report here is for
the in-app purchase event.

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:42.560
And you can see we have,
through a series of graphs,

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:47.210
the number of times this event
occurred in the last 30 days.

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:48.770
We can see the number
of unique users

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:51.080
or I suppose more
accurately app instances

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:53.210
that have performed this event.

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:55.310
We can see average
events per user,

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:56.870
and then the total
value of purchases

00:06:56.870 --> 00:06:59.150
during this time period.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:02.090
We also have a few custom
reports for net purchases.

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:04.430
In this case, we can see
what the top products are

00:07:04.430 --> 00:07:07.160
that our users have purchased,
what our average purchase

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:08.405
price per item was.

00:07:08.405 --> 00:07:10.280
And then if we scroll
down a bit you can see,

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:12.238
again, we get some nice
demographic information

00:07:12.238 --> 00:07:14.720
about the users who are
performing these events,

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:16.640
as well as an events
per session graph, which

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:18.657
sometimes comes in useful.

00:07:18.657 --> 00:07:20.490
And then you can see
we have similar reports

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:21.850
for our level-up event.

00:07:21.850 --> 00:07:23.880
This is one of those
suggested events

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:25.740
that Firebase
Analytics already has

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:29.190
predefined for game developers.

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:31.170
And also an unlock
achievement event.

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:34.620
This is a custom event that the
developers of our sample game

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.705
Bingo Blast have
created on their own.

00:07:37.705 --> 00:07:39.080
And so we can
learn a fair amount

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:41.300
just by looking at
these stock reports.

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:43.355
Like, my in-app
purchase report will

00:07:43.355 --> 00:07:46.080
let me know what products
are most popular.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.050
And I can do things
like filter this report

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:49.850
by some of our user properties.

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:52.430
For instance, I can filter
our in-app purchase event

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:54.770
by operating system version.

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:57.230
And that can start to inform
some important decisions

00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:00.120
like how backwards
compatible do we want to be?

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:02.960
Like, if it turns out
users who are on OS 8

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:05.240
are still spending money
within our game, well,

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:07.239
then maybe that makes
sense to kind of make sure

00:08:07.239 --> 00:08:09.110
that we can still
support those users.

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:11.150
And we're able to give
you these kind of reports

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:14.480
for no cost, no matter how many
millions upon millions of users

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:16.460
your app might have.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:18.237
So maybe this sounds
too good to be true

00:08:18.237 --> 00:08:19.820
and you're thinking
what the catch is,

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:22.070
and I'm glad you're silently
thinking that to yourself

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:24.200
because that's the rest
of this presentation.

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:26.120
So I want you take a
look at this graph again

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:26.985
or this report.

00:08:26.985 --> 00:08:29.360
You can see that our in-app
purchase event gives you some

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:32.570
very nice information about some
of the custom event parameters

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:33.470
that it gets in--

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:35.780
in our case, the product
that was purchased

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:38.760
and how much those items cost.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:42.470
You also see, for a predefined
event like our level-up event,

00:08:42.470 --> 00:08:44.720
we get a nice little
bar chart that shows you

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.850
what new level our
users have reached

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:49.240
when they reach this event.

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:52.160
But then if we go back to our
unlock achievement event here--

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:53.480
and this is a custom event--

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:56.420
we don't see any information
about the actual achievement

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:57.860
that our user has unlocked.

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:00.530
Like right now if we want to
know what achievements are most

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:03.080
popular, or which ones
are harder to unlock

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:05.480
than we've anticipated,
or get any information

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:08.390
about these parameters that are
sent with this customer report,

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:10.310
we currently can't get
any of that information

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:11.580
with this report.

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:13.400
And basically here's the deal.

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:15.380
The way Firebase
Analytics works is

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:17.210
that every day the
service gets millions

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:20.090
upon millions of events
for all of your apps,

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:22.970
and every few hours they
process this raw data into these

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:26.600
generated reports for you
to view at your leisure.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:29.570
And while these reports
are kept around for you

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:33.680
to view whenever you want,
this raw data is not,

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:35.780
which means that if you
want to run complex ad

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:40.220
hoc queries on historical data,
or export your data to use

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:43.089
with other analytics
packages, or simply run custom

00:09:43.089 --> 00:09:45.380
reports that you don't get
out of the box with Firebase

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:48.990
Analytics, you're going to
need access to that raw data.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:50.780
And that's where
BigQuery comes in.

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:52.700
I'm guessing here at
a Google Cloud event

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:55.370
I don't need to give you a whole
lot of detail on what BigQuery

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:57.890
is, but for those of
you watching at home,

00:09:57.890 --> 00:09:59.990
it's Google's data warehouse
in the cloud that's

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:02.360
designed to run very
fast queries over very

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:03.740
large amounts of data--

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:06.550
data like the kind of
analytics event data

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:09.612
an app with a whole bunch
of users might generate.

00:10:09.612 --> 00:10:12.070
And so the idea is that when
you link your Firebase account

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:15.040
with BigQuery, which you can do
through this handy little panel

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:16.207
in the Firebase console--

00:10:16.207 --> 00:10:17.040
it's under Settings.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:18.040
It's a little gear icon.

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:19.816
You can look for it--

00:10:19.816 --> 00:10:21.940
then when all this analytics
data from your clients

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:25.180
gets sent to Firebase
Analytics, a copy of that data

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:29.180
also gets sent to BigQuery, and
this happens in near real time.

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:32.320
BigQuery stores this data in
sort of a temporary intraday

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:34.630
table, and then at the
end of the day copies

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:36.160
it over to a more
permanent table

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:38.400
that you can keep for
as long as you'd like.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:40.150
And so you can query
these intraday tables

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:43.000
if you want something
resembling real-time analytics,

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:46.180
or it can look at
these historical tables

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:50.374
if you want to look at
historical data over time.

00:10:50.374 --> 00:10:52.540
Now, I should note that if
you want to use BigQuery,

00:10:52.540 --> 00:10:54.000
it's not entirely free.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:56.590
You will need to upgrade your
Firebase project to the Blaze

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:57.472
pay-as-you-go plan.

00:10:57.472 --> 00:10:58.930
And on the BigQuery
side of things,

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:01.000
you will need to pay
for the data you store,

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:03.160
the cost of the real-time
streaming inserts,

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:05.530
and the usage.

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:08.470
However, if we were to take
a look at a sample game like

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:11.650
Bingo Blast, which right now
has about 20,000 monthly active

00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:15.880
users across iOS and Android,
records 70 different event

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:18.430
types and 10 different
user properties,

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:21.490
right now our data storage
charges come up to about $0.60

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:25.420
a month to store the previous
10 months' worth of data.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:27.070
We're also paying
about $0.12 month

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:30.040
to insert that data into
BigQuery in real time.

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:32.590
And then if we were to run
a simple query across say,

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:35.650
a month's worth of Android
data, that costs about 1/10

00:11:35.650 --> 00:11:36.400
of a penny.

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:38.710
If we were to run 50
queries like that every day

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:41.620
across both Android and iOS,
we'd be looking at about $3

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:42.584
a month.

00:11:42.584 --> 00:11:44.500
However, it turns out
that BigQuery offers you

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:47.650
one terabyte of usage
data for free every month,

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:50.050
and this falls well
under that free tier.

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:52.360
So this part actually
costs nothing,

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:54.400
for a grand total of $0.72.

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:56.500
Now obviously huge
disclaimer here.

00:11:56.500 --> 00:11:58.390
These figures can
vary considerably,

00:11:58.390 --> 00:12:00.970
depending on how much data
you're collecting, how long you

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:02.950
keep it around
for, and how often

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:05.140
and how complex you're
making these queries.

00:12:05.140 --> 00:12:07.760
But at least for our sample
app, linking to BigQuery

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:09.850
did not involve a
huge financial hit.

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:12.820
And I should also note that
there are some tools in place

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:15.550
to set quotas or alert
you if pricing goes over

00:12:15.550 --> 00:12:17.884
what you're expecting.

00:12:17.884 --> 00:12:19.300
So looking back
at our sample app,

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:20.916
there are a few
questions that I want

00:12:20.916 --> 00:12:23.290
to be able to answer that I
can't right now from Firebase

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:23.920
Analytics.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:25.420
First, give me some
more information

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:27.070
about what achievements
were actually

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:30.250
unlocked by this unlock
achievement method-- event.

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:32.530
Second, while it's nice
that I can filter my events

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:35.110
by a single user
property, I'm actually

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:37.246
interested in filtering
by two user properties.

00:12:37.246 --> 00:12:38.620
Like, for example,
what if I want

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:40.750
to see our spend
virtual currency

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:44.170
event for our users
who speak English

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:47.650
and also have power ups?

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:49.990
Filtering by two user
properties at once

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:52.450
is not something I can do right
now with the out of the box

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:53.845
reports.

00:12:53.845 --> 00:12:55.970
Third, I want to generate
a few custom reports that

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:59.720
span both my Android and my
iOS data, and in theory maybe

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:01.850
I want to combine this
data with data coming in

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:04.569
from another analytics
tool that my app is using.

00:13:04.569 --> 00:13:06.110
And then fourth, I
want to see what's

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:09.680
causing my users to spend
virtual currency in my game.

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:12.320
Sure, I can see reports on
this spend virtual currency

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:13.410
event itself.

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:15.780
But what are the events
that lead up to that event?

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:18.155
That's something that would
be pretty valuable data to me

00:13:18.155 --> 00:13:21.060
as someone who wants my
users to spend money.

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:23.990
So these are all areas where
analyzing the data in BigQuery

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:24.951
might help.

00:13:24.951 --> 00:13:27.200
And now before we do that,
before we get in the demos,

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:29.390
I do want to explain what our
data is going to look like.

00:13:29.390 --> 00:13:31.520
Because I don't know
about you, but I sometimes

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:34.020
think of BigQuery as
a giant SQL table.

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:36.860
And if you've played with
any of our public sample data

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:40.640
like New York City Taxi
rides or Hacker News posts,

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:42.830
this is kind of
what it looks like.

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:44.800
And maybe you knew
this, but I didn't.

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:47.180
BigQuery is really much more
sophisticated than this.

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:49.520
The rows in BigQuery aren't
really single rows that

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:51.830
consist of one line of data.

00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:54.890
They're more like individual
JSON trees that are all

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:56.930
crammed into each
row, each of which

00:13:56.930 --> 00:13:59.630
can contain key value pairs
and arrays and objects

00:13:59.630 --> 00:14:00.824
and arrays of objects.

00:14:00.824 --> 00:14:02.240
And that's important
to understand

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:04.771
when you're looking at
Firebase Analytics in BigQuery,

00:14:04.771 --> 00:14:06.770
because one of the first
things you might assume

00:14:06.770 --> 00:14:08.660
is that each row in
BigQuery corresponds

00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:10.940
to a single Firebase
Analytics event,

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:12.770
and that's actually
not the case.

00:14:12.770 --> 00:14:14.690
More often, a row
in BigQuery will

00:14:14.690 --> 00:14:17.270
consist of a set of
user information,

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:19.070
and then probably
two or three events

00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:22.130
bundled together for that user.

00:14:22.130 --> 00:14:24.890
So let me explain by showing
you what our actual data

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:26.640
scheme looks like.

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:29.180
So our user info
section here contains

00:14:29.180 --> 00:14:33.050
a few straightforward key value
pairs like first open time

00:14:33.050 --> 00:14:36.350
and some kind of unique ID
that you as an app developer

00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:37.994
might provide.

00:14:37.994 --> 00:14:40.160
But then they also obtain
a number of nested objects

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:42.320
that all have their own
set of key value pairs,

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:44.960
things like the device
that the user is using,

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:48.440
their geographic location, the
traffic source information--

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:52.270
what marketing campaign brought
them into your app, and so on.

00:14:52.270 --> 00:14:54.140
We also store user
properties that we

00:14:54.140 --> 00:14:56.940
have associated with this
user in the Firebase Analytics

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:57.594
library.

00:14:57.594 --> 00:14:59.010
And these properties
are basically

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:01.140
stored as an array of objects.

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:04.170
These objects in turn
contain the name of the key

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:05.250
as well as the value.

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:07.020
And the value
itself is an object

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:10.414
that has an end value, a string
value, double value, and so on.

00:15:10.414 --> 00:15:12.330
And you'll find that in
practice most of these

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:13.920
are null, except for
the property that

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:16.650
contains the actual value
you're interested in.

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:18.210
This basically
allows you to store

00:15:18.210 --> 00:15:20.850
any number of user
properties in any format,

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:23.010
and they're kept inside
your BigQuery table

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:24.420
in a consistent manner.

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:26.820
And the nice thing about
keeping your user properties

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:29.571
in a big nested array like
this is that there's basically

00:15:29.571 --> 00:15:32.070
one less join that you need to
worry about later when you're

00:15:32.070 --> 00:15:33.111
building up your queries.

00:15:33.111 --> 00:15:36.319
And you'll start to see that
in the demo in a little bit.

00:15:36.319 --> 00:15:38.360
So then in addition to
all this user information,

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:40.130
there's some event information.

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:41.810
And as I mentioned
earlier, BigQuery

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:43.760
tends to store these
Firebase Analytics events

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:46.580
as an array of three or four
event objects all bundled

00:15:46.580 --> 00:15:48.499
together in the same row.

00:15:48.499 --> 00:15:50.540
These also contain some
straightforward key value

00:15:50.540 --> 00:15:52.498
pairs, like the name of
the event, and the time

00:15:52.498 --> 00:15:54.800
stamp when this event
occurred within your app.

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:57.620
But then they also contain
an array of event parameters.

00:15:57.620 --> 00:15:59.720
And just like user
properties, these parameters

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:00.869
have keys and values.

00:16:00.869 --> 00:16:03.410
And these values themselves are
their own little objects that

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:06.532
contain string value, end
value, double value, and so on.

00:16:06.532 --> 00:16:08.240
And that's pretty much
the data structure

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:10.240
that you're going to be
dealing with in Firebase

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:12.146
Analytics in BigQuery.

00:16:12.146 --> 00:16:14.270
So with that in mind, let's
revisit my initial list

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:16.820
of questions that I couldn't get
answered by Firebase Analytics

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:17.150
alone.

00:16:17.150 --> 00:16:18.800
And we'll start with
that first question,

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:20.758
what achievements are
actually getting unlocked

00:16:20.758 --> 00:16:21.876
with this event?

00:16:21.876 --> 00:16:23.750
This is something our
game design team really

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:24.920
wants to know.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:26.817
So luckily, querying
custom parameters

00:16:26.817 --> 00:16:28.400
like this within
BigQuery is something

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:31.010
that really any halfway
decent SQL developer

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:32.150
could put together.

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:34.250
Turns out, though, I
am not that developer.

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:36.260
Honestly, I barely know
what I'm doing up here.

00:16:36.260 --> 00:16:38.570
Luckily, I'm here
with Arun, who does.

00:16:38.570 --> 00:16:41.210
So he's going to walk us through
some live examples on how

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:43.760
to perform some of these queries
using some actual Firebase

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:45.260
Analytics data in BigQuery.

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:47.180
So Arun, why don't
you take it away?

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:48.960
ARUN VENKATESAN: Thanks, Todd.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:50.450
Hi, everybody.

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:53.120
So in order to answer Todd's
questions of which achievements

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:55.340
are getting unlocked in
the app, let's actually

00:16:55.340 --> 00:16:58.730
see some Android code that
you need to implement.

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:02.240
The Android snippet here shows
us the unlock achievement event

00:17:02.240 --> 00:17:04.520
being added to
Firebase, with the name

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:08.990
of the achievement, coins100,
being added as a custom string

00:17:08.990 --> 00:17:10.190
parameter.

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:13.760
Similarly, when the user clicks
other achievements in your app,

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:16.250
such as user power
ups, daubs and coins,

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:20.730
you can add those as a
achievements names as well.

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:22.500
A few slides earlier,
Todd showed us

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:25.369
the scheme of a Firebase
record in BigQuery.

00:17:25.369 --> 00:17:27.839
The Firebase event and
the parameter names

00:17:27.839 --> 00:17:30.600
that we just logged
in Android snippet

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:33.540
are shown in BigQuery
record, as seen here.

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:36.570
The unlock
achievement event name

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:39.720
is stored as a value in the
first events name field,

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:41.460
and the corresponding
parameter is

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:44.760
stored as a value in the nested
parameter string value field.

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:48.720
Let's now do a quick demo.

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:56.060
So this is BigQuery
SQL editor, and we're

00:17:56.060 --> 00:18:00.282
going to use standard SQL
for all our examples today.

00:18:00.282 --> 00:18:01.740
The query results
show us the count

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:04.355
of each unlocked achievement,
indicating the most popular

00:18:04.355 --> 00:18:05.355
achievements in the app.

00:18:05.355 --> 00:18:09.670
This is just the results that
we just ran a minute ago.

00:18:09.670 --> 00:18:11.545
As seen here, the top
achievements in the app

00:18:11.545 --> 00:18:15.740
are coins 500 and daub, both
of which are equally popular.

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:21.630
Let's now look at the
actual query itself.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:24.010
Note the unnest syntax here.

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:26.000
In the from class,
the unnest, it

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:28.630
threads through an array
of Firebase objects, which

00:18:28.630 --> 00:18:31.990
are event objects in this case,
stored in the event dim record,

00:18:31.990 --> 00:18:34.000
and produces one
row for each event.

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:37.340
That's just a convenience
function for you.

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:39.340
Let's move on to
another example.

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:40.960
Earlier in the
presentation, Todd

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:45.500
showed you the events report
in Firebase Analytics console.

00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:47.960
Here is how we can generate
the same report in BigQuery.

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:08.770
As seen here, the results
show the top events

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:10.267
and their even counts.

00:19:10.267 --> 00:19:11.850
The spend virtual
currency is actually

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:16.340
a second most popular event
in the app, which is great.

00:19:16.340 --> 00:19:18.980
Let's look at our
other open questions.

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:21.410
The [AUDIO OUT] asked
about filtering events

00:19:21.410 --> 00:19:24.540
by multiple user properties.

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:27.630
In our example, we will filter
the spend virtual currency

00:19:27.630 --> 00:19:30.030
event by the device
language and powers,

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:31.760
which are both user properties.

00:19:50.770 --> 00:19:54.130
The result shows that users with
device language set to English

00:19:54.130 --> 00:19:56.230
and had power ups
in the app spent

00:19:56.230 --> 00:19:59.890
$2,250 virtual dollars in
the app for a total of 218

00:19:59.890 --> 00:20:03.310
times, which is great.

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:05.920
Here is the query
that we just executed.

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:07.780
The query is in two parts.

00:20:07.780 --> 00:20:11.020
In the first part of the query,
we apply the max function

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:12.920
to all of the rows so
that user properties

00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:15.670
are applied uniformly to all
bundles of events for the given

00:20:15.670 --> 00:20:17.540
user.

00:20:17.540 --> 00:20:19.370
And in the second
part of the query

00:20:19.370 --> 00:20:21.380
we take the results
from the inner query

00:20:21.380 --> 00:20:23.810
and only select those
events for which both

00:20:23.810 --> 00:20:25.910
the user property
criteria are satisfied,

00:20:25.910 --> 00:20:29.590
the power ups and the language.

00:20:29.590 --> 00:20:32.920
Let us now look at
other open questions.

00:20:32.920 --> 00:20:35.520
Let's now see how to combine
our iOS and Android data.

00:20:42.400 --> 00:20:43.939
This is a cross-device query.

00:20:43.939 --> 00:20:44.980
And we'll shortly see it.

00:20:57.420 --> 00:21:01.650
The results show that while iOS
tablet users a greater than iOS

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:03.600
mobile device users,
the results are just

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:07.940
the opposite for Android
users, which is interesting.

00:21:07.940 --> 00:21:10.130
Here is the query
that we just ran.

00:21:10.130 --> 00:21:12.800
It shows how the data from
two different data sources

00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:15.230
can be combined in BigQuery.

00:21:15.230 --> 00:21:18.440
In this example, notice
how we not only join

00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:21.750
data across our iOS and
Android apps, but in addition,

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:23.840
we also joined the iOS
and Android app data

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:27.337
across multiple dates.

00:21:27.337 --> 00:21:28.920
Now, it turns out
that our sample data

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:32.410
us from a purely mobile
app, but in some cases

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:34.459
you might also have a
web app, in which case

00:21:34.459 --> 00:21:37.000
you're probably tracking your
analytics in a tool like Google

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:38.350
Analytics.

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:40.180
It turns out that
with BigQuery you

00:21:40.180 --> 00:21:43.630
can combine data from Firebase
Analytics with other providers,

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:46.630
such as the data that you
get from Google Analytics.

00:21:46.630 --> 00:21:50.800
You can join your iOS, Android,
and your web data in BigQuery

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:55.670
to determine user behavior
across all of your apps.

00:21:55.670 --> 00:21:57.920
Let's now look at probably
the most important question

00:21:57.920 --> 00:22:00.630
in our minds, which is on
how you might figure out

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:04.100
what's causing users to
spend money in your app.

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:06.060
In order to answer
this question,

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:07.670
we will build a
sequence of events

00:22:07.670 --> 00:22:10.970
by user that leads up to
the user actually spending

00:22:10.970 --> 00:22:14.140
money in your app, what we might
call a custom event funnel.

00:22:16.810 --> 00:22:20.560
Here is a query that generates
a sequence of three events.

00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:23.170
You can modify this query to
include any number of events

00:22:23.170 --> 00:22:25.550
you want in the funnel.

00:22:25.550 --> 00:22:28.140
The partition by class
breaks the input rows

00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:31.360
into separate partitions
by app instance ID,

00:22:31.360 --> 00:22:34.150
which is a proxy for
user ID in this case.

00:22:34.150 --> 00:22:38.010
And the lead navigation
function returns the next event

00:22:38.010 --> 00:22:40.650
based on an ordering
criteria, which in our case

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:44.190
is the event time stamp, thus
creating a series of events

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:45.680
ordered by time stamp.

00:22:48.970 --> 00:22:50.570
The results for part
one of our query

00:22:50.570 --> 00:22:54.510
shows the three events
for a given user.

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:57.720
The user IDs have been
masked for privacy reasons.

00:22:57.720 --> 00:22:59.630
As noticed in the
interim result,

00:22:59.630 --> 00:23:01.880
the second event
in the first row

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:06.490
appears as the first event
in the next row and so on.

00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:08.710
Let's now look at
part two of the query.

00:23:08.710 --> 00:23:12.500
We'll apply results from part
one to part two of the query.

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:15.850
In the second part of our query,
we simply define our end event

00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:17.680
as the spend virtual
currency event,

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:20.470
since we would like to determine
what users did before they

00:23:20.470 --> 00:23:22.480
spent money in the app.

00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:25.630
This query calculates the number
of users and the frequency

00:23:25.630 --> 00:23:28.810
with which this funnel
has been navigated.

00:23:28.810 --> 00:23:30.550
Let's look at the results.

00:23:30.550 --> 00:23:34.430
And it shows you what makes
users spend money in their app.

00:23:34.430 --> 00:23:36.180
And you can see all
of the different paths

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:39.560
that users took before they
spent money on your app.

00:23:39.560 --> 00:23:41.900
For instance, a lot
of users spent money

00:23:41.900 --> 00:23:45.140
after completing a
round, which is great.

00:23:45.140 --> 00:23:48.470
On the other hand, it also
shows you that you the developer

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:51.230
could do more to entice
your users to spend money

00:23:51.230 --> 00:23:55.080
after spinning a slot
or after leveling up.

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:57.310
All good so far, except
that the product folks

00:23:57.310 --> 00:24:01.190
don't want to deal with
SQL, so what's the solution?

00:24:01.190 --> 00:24:03.220
Google Data Studio.

00:24:03.220 --> 00:24:05.345
Google Data Studio
turns your raw analytics

00:24:05.345 --> 00:24:09.680
file-based data into informative
reports that are easy to read,

00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:12.120
share, and fully customizable.

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:14.980
You can now create no-cost
and unlimited charts

00:24:14.980 --> 00:24:16.610
and other interactive
visualizations.

00:24:24.280 --> 00:24:25.900
In order to help
you get started,

00:24:25.900 --> 00:24:28.210
we provide two
sample reports that

00:24:28.210 --> 00:24:31.300
are based on a comprehensive
set of metrics and dimensions

00:24:31.300 --> 00:24:33.850
available throughout
your BigQuery schema.

00:24:33.850 --> 00:24:35.710
You can use these
samples as templates

00:24:35.710 --> 00:24:38.110
to quickly create their own
reports and visualizations

00:24:38.110 --> 00:24:39.820
based on your own
app's raw data.

00:24:39.820 --> 00:24:41.730
And let's see how to do it.

00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:44.430
We'll now use the events
sample report in our demo.

00:24:47.549 --> 00:24:49.590
So let's first take a look
at some of the charts.

00:24:49.590 --> 00:24:53.300
So there's some basic dashboards
and then reports based

00:24:53.300 --> 00:24:56.860
on events and also conversions.

00:24:56.860 --> 00:25:00.120
This is all in
the sample report.

00:25:00.120 --> 00:25:06.390
Let's use this template,
create a new data source,

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:13.360
specify our BigQuery project,
select the Android data set,

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:14.785
and select events.

00:25:17.241 --> 00:25:19.490
Once we connect this data,
we'll add it to the report.

00:25:31.920 --> 00:25:32.710
There you go.

00:25:32.710 --> 00:25:35.340
So this was your
sample data here,

00:25:35.340 --> 00:25:37.910
and this was your own
data, but generated

00:25:37.910 --> 00:25:39.750
with the same reports.

00:25:39.750 --> 00:25:41.790
You did not have to
type or enter anything.

00:25:41.790 --> 00:25:43.530
You just had to go
through a few clicks.

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:45.090
It's that intuitive
and that simple.

00:25:54.380 --> 00:25:56.810
Google Data Studio also
lets you create reports

00:25:56.810 --> 00:26:00.530
based on custom SQL queries,
and we'll see how to do it.

00:26:00.530 --> 00:26:03.320
We'll create a report using the
events report query that we saw

00:26:03.320 --> 00:26:04.670
earlier in the presentation.

00:26:08.430 --> 00:26:10.430
So I'm going to copy the
SQL query that we wrote

00:26:10.430 --> 00:26:14.930
for the events report, and
in the Google Data Studio

00:26:14.930 --> 00:26:18.380
homepage, select Data
Sources on the left.

00:26:18.380 --> 00:26:21.940
Select to create
a new data source.

00:26:21.940 --> 00:26:24.130
Specify a Custom Query.

00:26:24.130 --> 00:26:26.827
And select your data set.

00:26:26.827 --> 00:26:28.410
I'm pasting the query
here that I just

00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:32.940
ran in the BigQuery SQL editor.

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:35.370
And it's a standard SQL,
so take off legacy SQL.

00:26:39.650 --> 00:26:40.810
And I create a report.

00:26:48.920 --> 00:26:50.970
Once I've ordered this
data to the report,

00:26:50.970 --> 00:26:53.710
I can now create nice charts.

00:26:53.710 --> 00:26:58.529
So I'm going to do a pie
chart for the events.

00:26:58.529 --> 00:26:59.570
Let's see how that works.

00:27:02.890 --> 00:27:04.960
There you go.

00:27:04.960 --> 00:27:07.710
So with just a few clicks
you have a nice pie chart,

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:11.240
which will show you a nice
visual illustration of all

00:27:11.240 --> 00:27:14.430
your events and the event
count, thus making your product

00:27:14.430 --> 00:27:15.550
and marketing folks happy.

00:27:21.090 --> 00:27:24.760
To recap, Firebase Analytics
is at the heart of Firebase

00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:26.920
and is a no-cost
and powerful tool

00:27:26.920 --> 00:27:29.530
to meet all of your
mobile analytics needs.

00:27:29.530 --> 00:27:31.720
Combined with BigQuery
and Data Studio,

00:27:31.720 --> 00:27:34.090
you have a powerful analytics
platform and querying

00:27:34.090 --> 00:27:35.900
engine in your hands.

00:27:35.900 --> 00:27:37.660
Thank you.

